                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The Night Christmas Ended is one of the most ridiculous Christmas games I have played, and I mean that as a compliment. It throws me into the role of a giant goblin on a bizarre adventure involving evil pine trees, ridiculous enemies and an extremely strange version of the holiday season. I liked the fact that the game never takes its story seriously because the complete absurdity is what gives it so much personality.\n\nThe gameplay is a fast arcade shoot em up where I have to deal with enemies while constantly moving around the screen. There are different weapons and power ups that can dramatically change how I approach each section. I enjoyed how simple the controls are because the game clearly wants me to focus on surviving the chaos rather than learning a complicated control scheme.\n\nThe power ups are especially fun because some of them are completely ridiculous. Shots can change size and behave in unexpected ways, while different weapons give me ways to handle the enemies more efficiently. I also like that the giant goblin comments on how I am playing, which adds another layer of comedy to an already ridiculous adventure.\n\nThe variety of situations keeps the campaign entertaining. I can fight strange enemies on the ground, deal with airborne sections and even end up in space. I liked that the game keeps changing what is happening instead of staying in one ordinary arena. The pixel art and old school arcade presentation also fit the chaotic design perfectly.\n\nMy biggest issue is that the game clearly shows its age. Some of the controls and presentation feel rough compared with modern arcade shooters, and certain sections can be frustrating when the action becomes especially busy. The comedy can also be very immature, so I do not think everyone will enjoy its particular style of humor.\n\nThe local cooperative mode is easily one of the best features for me. Playing through the adventure with another person makes the ridiculous situations even more entertaining, especially when both of us are trying to survive the same chaotic screen. It gives the game more value than I think the short campaign would have on its own.\n\nOverall, The Night Christmas Ended is a silly and entertaining arcade shooter that I enjoyed for its complete lack of restraint. I liked the strange enemies, ridiculous story, unusual power ups and especially the cooperative mode. It is rough around the edges and very dependent on its particular sense of humor, but the whole experience has enough personality to make it memorable.\n\nRating: 6.5/10\n",
